ROCR-Runtime (pinned commit 2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350,
therock-7.14 / ROCm 7.14.0): add the missing _mm_sfence() to
SdmaQueue::RingDoorbell()'s legacy-doorbell publish sequence, fixing an
intermittent hang in any hipMemcpyWithStream (or other SDMA-routed async
copy) on gfx803.

WHY
---
Reproduced via a real vLLM workload (Qwen3.5-2B load, torch.compile's
_sfdp_init doing a small .to(device) tensor copy) hanging non-
deterministically: identical retries sometimes hung at this exact call,
sometimes sailed through it and hung nowhere else. py-spy --native on the
stuck process showed the host spinning in
rocr::core::BusyWaitSignal::WaitRelaxed -> hsa_signal_wait_scacquire,
reached via hipMemcpyWithStream, forever -- `rocm-smi --showuse` read 0%
GPU busy the whole time and dmesg logged no fault, ring hang, or VM
fault. So the doorbell write was reaching the queue submission path (no
enumeration/setup failure) but the GPU never signaled completion for that
submission: consistent with the SDMA engine picking up a wptr/doorbell
update before the packet descriptor it names was actually visible in
memory, and either faulting silently, dropping the packet, or stalling
on a corrupt descriptor -- with no hardware-visible fault.

SdmaQueue::RingDoorbell (amd_sdma_queue.cpp) publishes a submission with:

    atomic::Store(&amd_queue_.write_dispatch_id, write_index, release);
    atomic::Store(queue_wptr_, write_index, release);
    std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release);
    *queue_doorbell_ = write_index;

std::atomic_thread_fence(release) is a compiler barrier plus, on x86, an
ordinary store fence -- per the x86 SDM (11.3.1), it does not order or
drain write-combining (WC) stores, which are explicitly exempted from
the normal store-ordering guarantees other memory types get for free.
queue_start_addr_ (the ring the caller wrote the SDMA copy descriptor
into, allocated via GpuAgent::system_allocator()/coarsegrain_allocator()
with AllocateExecutable, same as the AQL ring buffer) and queue_wptr_ are
WC-mapped GPU-visible host memory. Only SFENCE (or MFENCE) actually
orders WC stores against a later UC/strongly-ordered store such as the
doorbell BAR write immediately below it.

This is the identical bug class this repo already root-caused and fixed
once, in the neighboring AQL compute-queue path: see
hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch's 2026-08-24 UPDATE, where
AqlQueue::StoreRelaxed's legacy-doorbell branch was found to be missing
the same _mm_sfence() the type-2 (DoorbellType==2) branch already has,
root-caused via a stuck HIP-graph-replay repro and a live MQD register
dump. That fix's own comment states the type-2 path calls _mm_sfence()
specifically "to drain the CPU's write-combining buffers for the AQL
ring memory before the ... doorbell BAR write executes" -- the exact
same hazard applies to SdmaQueue::RingDoorbell, which was never given
the equivalent fix because it is a separate class from AqlQueue and
wasn't touched by that patch.

The non-determinism itself is diagnostic: a hard, always-reproducing
deadlock (like the graph-replay batch-chunk bug this repo also fixed)
would hang at the identical byte offset every run. A missing-fence race
depends on when the CPU's WC buffer happens to flush for unrelated
reasons (cache pressure, an intervening serializing instruction, timing
noise) -- which matches the observed behavior exactly: reproduced
hanging at this call on one run, then completing torch.compile cleanly
on an immediate retry with no code change.

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE as of this patch text beyond the
initial reproduction runs above -- see tools/rocr-novad-rebuild/ for the
standalone ROCR-Runtime rebuild used to test this without a full image
rebuild, and vllm-gfx803/NOTES.md for the verification result once run
(repeated retries needed, since the bug is intermittent -- a single
clean run does not confirm the fix any more than a single hang confirmed
the bug).

WHAT
----
- amd_sdma_queue.cpp: add _mm_sfence() in SdmaQueue::RingDoorbell(),
  immediately before the doorbell write, after the existing (necessary
  but insufficient) std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release).

Applies to: ROCR-Runtime @ rocm-systems commit 2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350
(therock-7.14 / ROCm 7.14.0 pin). Not gfx803-specific in the sense of
touching arch-gated code -- generic SDMA dispatch-protocol logic on any
board whose queue/doorbell memory is WC-mapped. gfx803 is simply where
this repo found it, via the legacy-doorbell board this repo already
carries the sibling AQL fix for.

diff --git a/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_sdma_queue.cpp b/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_sdma_queue.cpp
index 1111111111..2222222222 100644
--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_sdma_queue.cpp
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_sdma_queue.cpp
@@ -222,6 +222,12 @@ hsa_status_t SdmaQueue::RingDoorbell(uint64_t write_index) {
   atomic::Store(&amd_queue_.write_dispatch_id, write_index, std::memory_order_release);
   atomic::Store(queue_wptr_, write_index, std::memory_order_release);
   std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release);
+  // A release fence is a compiler barrier plus, on x86, an ordinary store
+  // fence -- it does not drain the write-combining buffers that
+  // queue_start_addr_/queue_wptr_ (WC-mapped GPU-visible host memory) go
+  // through. Only SFENCE orders WC stores against the doorbell write below,
+  // same reasoning as amd_aql_queue.cpp's StoreRelaxed type-2 path.
+  _mm_sfence();
   *queue_doorbell_ = write_index;

